        <!-- SECTION 4: Connecting to Natural Gas: What It Takes -->
        <div id="natural-gas-hookup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Connecting to Natural Gas: What It Takes
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the part most buyers underestimate, and it is worth understanding before you buy rather than after.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You Need a Plumbed Connection Point</h3>
          <p>
            A generator cannot simply be connected to a barbecue quick-connect and expected to run — a large generator's gas demand is many times a grill's. What you need is a properly sized gas line run to an outdoor quick-connect fitting near where the generator will sit, installed by a licensed gas fitter. Typical cost is $400-900 depending on distance from the meter and how much of the run is exposed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Line Sizing and Pressure Matter</h3>
          <p>
            A 13,000-watt generator can consume 200-300 cubic feet of gas per hour, comparable to a furnace and water heater running simultaneously. The supply line must be sized for that demand at the required pressure — typically 5 to 11 inches of water column — over the actual run length. An undersized line produces a generator that starts fine and then stumbles under load, which is a maddening problem to diagnose after the fact. Give your gas fitter the generator's specification sheet, not a general description.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check Meter Capacity First</h3>
          <p>
            Adding a generator's demand on top of an existing furnace, water heater, range, and dryer can exceed what an older meter can supply. Your utility can tell you the meter's capacity and what your existing appliances consume. If an upgrade is needed, the utility typically handles it, sometimes at no charge and sometimes with a fee and a scheduling delay of weeks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Permits and Inspection</h3>
          <p>
            Most jurisdictions require a permit for a new gas line, with an inspection and a pressure test. This is worth doing properly — an unpermitted gas line is an insurance problem and a disclosure problem at sale, and gas leaks are not a category where improvisation pays.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the Right Hose</h3>
          <p>
            The connection between the quick-connect and the generator must be a listed flexible gas connector rated for the pressure and flow, not a barbecue hose. Most tri-fuel generators ship with an appropriate hose; if yours does not, buy one rated for the application rather than adapting something.
          </p>
        </div>

