          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Starting Watts Are What Trip You</h3>
          <p>
            Motor loads surge on startup — a one-horsepower well pump running at 1,200 watts can demand 4,000 for a second. Add the running watts of everything on simultaneously, then confirm the natural gas starting rating covers your largest motor's surge on top of that. This is where the 22% derate bites hardest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Transfer Switch Is Essential Here</h3>
          <p>
            A generator plumbed to a fixed gas connection is effectively a permanent installation, which means running extension cords through a window makes no sense. Install a manual transfer switch or an interlock kit so the generator can power hardwired circuits — furnace, well pump, lighting — which is most of what matters. Budget $500-1,500 installed.
          </p>
