                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gasoline</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$3-5</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$72-120</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Propane</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$5-8</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$120-190</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Natural gas is typically the cheapest by a wide margin, and it is also the only one with no acquisition effort attached — no cans to fill, no tanks to swap, no queue at a station that may not have power to pump. Propane is usually the most expensive per delivered kilowatt-hour, particularly when bought through exchange cages that fill tanks to 15 pounds rather than 20.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those figures assume a moderate household load rather than full output, and they move with local utility and fuel prices, so treat them as relative rather than absolute. The ranking, though, holds almost everywhere: natural gas cheapest, gasoline in the middle, propane dearest.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this should override the availability logic. Propane costs the most per hour and is still the fuel worth keeping in reserve, because it stores indefinitely and remains available when a regional emergency empties the gas stations. The right way to read this table is as a reason to run natural gas as the default whenever it is flowing — not as a reason to skip the backup fuels.
          </p>
