        <!-- SECTION 7: Safety and Maintenance -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Safety and Maintenance
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Every Fuel Produces Carbon Monoxide</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Natural gas and propane burn cleaner than gasoline but still produce lethal amounts of carbon monoxide. The 20-foot setback from windows, doors, and vents applies to all three fuels without exception, and a garage with the door open is not outdoors. Install battery-powered CO detectors inside the house regardless of where the generator sits.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Connection Safety</h3>
          <p>
            Soap-test the connection every time you attach the generator to the quick-connect, and inspect the flexible connector annually for cracking, abrasion, or corrosion at the fittings. Know where the gas shutoff for that line is, and shut it off when the generator is not connected. Never use a barbecue hose or an adapter chain to make a connection that was not designed for it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Exercise Monthly, on a Gaseous Fuel</h3>
          <p>
            The most common failure mode for a stored generator is a carburetor gummed by stale gasoline. Running the unit under load for 20-30 minutes monthly keeps everything working, and doing it on natural gas or propane means no varnish forms at all. With a plumbed connection this takes almost no effort, which is a quiet advantage of tri-fuel ownership.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep Gasoline Out of It Unless You Need It</h3>
          <p>
            Because natural gas and propane cover almost every scenario, many tri-fuel owners simply never put gasoline in the tank, which eliminates the stale fuel problem entirely. If you do use gasoline, stabilize it or run the carburetor bowl dry before storage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil and Air Filter</h3>
          <p>
            Change oil after the first 20-25 hours and every 50-100 hours thereafter. These engines hold roughly a quart, so degradation is fast and a missed change matters. Check the level before any extended run — the low-oil shutoff protects the engine but discovering it works during an outage is poor timing.
          </p>
        </div>

