                It costs far less — roughly $1,500-3,000 all in versus $9,000-13,000 installed for a standby unit — and connected to natural gas it offers the same unlimited runtime. What you give up is automation: you must go outside, start it, and switch circuits manually. For households willing to do that, the value is genuinely strong.
