        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes With Tri-Fuel Generators
          </h2>

          <p>
            The errors specific to this category almost all involve the natural gas side of the equation.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing Against the Gasoline Rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Natural gas costs you 20-25% of the rated output, and natural gas is the fuel you will actually run during a multi-day outage. Size against the natural gas figure — particularly the starting watt rating, which is where a well pump or AC compressor surge will find the limit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Connecting to an Undersized Gas Line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A large generator's demand is comparable to a furnace and water heater running together. An undersized line produces a unit that starts fine and then stumbles under load, which is maddening to diagnose afterward. Give your gas fitter the generator's specification sheet and have the line sized for the actual run length.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Barbecue Hose or Adapter Chain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The connection must be a listed flexible gas connector rated for the pressure and flow, not a grill hose adapted with fittings. This is a gas leak and fire risk, not a technicality. Use the hose supplied with the generator or buy one rated for the application.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Gas Service Never Fails</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Natural gas is more reliable than the electrical grid but not immune — earthquakes, major storms, and preemptive utility shutoffs all happen. Keep two or three propane tanks as backup, which costs little and preserves the whole point of buying a multi-fuel machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Positioning It Before Planning the Connections</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                These machines weigh 250 pounds or more, and once placed they stay placed. The location has to satisfy three things at once: proximity to the gas connection point, proximity to the transfer switch inlet, and at least 20 feet from windows and doors with the exhaust pointed away. Plan all three before delivery.
              </p>
